The question is about Marissa hiking a trail. She wants her average speed over the entire trail to be 4 miles per hour. To determine the speed she needs to walk the remaining distance, we need to consider the following:

1. Total distance of the trail: 12 miles (as deduced from the problem)
2. Distance already covered: 4 miles + 2 miles = 6 miles
3. Remaining distance: 12 miles - 6 miles = 6 miles
4. Time already taken: 1 hour + 1 hour = 2 hours
5. Total time required for an overall average speed of 4 miles per hour: 
   $$
   \text{Total time} = \frac{\text{Total distance}}{\text{Average speed}} = \frac{12 \text{ miles}}{4 \text{ miles/hour}} = 3 \text{ hours}
   $$
6. Time remaining for the remaining distance: 3 hours - 2 hours = 1 hour
7. Required speed for the remaining distance: 
   $$
   \text{Speed} = \frac{\text{Remaining distance}}{\text{Time remaining}} = \frac{6 \text{ miles}}{1 \text{ hour}} = 6 \text{ miles/hour}
   $$

Thus, the required speed is $\boxed{6}$.
